The [[b:Java Memory Model (JMM)]] defines which values a read is allowed to observe and which ordering guarantees exist between actions in different threads. Without a [[g:happens-before]] path, conflicting accesses form a data race; “it worked in a test” does not make their result portable.

Important happens-before edges include:

- program order within one thread
- monitor unlock → a subsequent lock of the same monitor
- write to a `volatile` field → every subsequent read of that field in the synchronization order
- actions before `Thread.start()` → actions in the started thread
- all actions in a thread → another thread successfully detects its termination through `join()`

Happens-before is transitive. It provides visibility and ordering for the actions before/after those edges; it is more precise than saying “flush the CPU cache.”

<pre><code><span class="k">class</span> Worker {
    <span class="k">private</span> <span class="k">int</span> data;
    <span class="k">private volatile boolean</span> stop;
    <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">shutdown</span>() {
        data = <span class="n">42</span>;
        stop = <span class="k">true</span>;       <span class="c">// release/publication edge</span>
    }
    <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">run</span>() {
        <span class="k">while</span> (!stop) Thread.<span class="f">onSpinWait</span>();
        System.out.<span class="f">println</span>(data); <span class="c">// 42 after observing stop == true</span>
    }
}</code></pre>

The volatile read that observes the published write also observes `data = 42`, which precedes that write. “Latest value” means a value permitted by the volatile synchronization order—not an unsynchronized wall-clock promise while writes race. Removing `volatile` permits the loop to keep reading an old value or be optimized in ways that never observe shutdown.

:::warn Visibility is not compound atomicity
`volatile int count; count++` is still a read, calculation and write that can lose updates. Use `AtomicInteger`/CAS, a lock, or ownership that removes the race. Volatile is appropriate for independent state such as a cancellation/config reference when all invariants fit one atomic read/write.
:::

:::deep
JITs map JMM guarantees to architecture-specific barriers and instructions; portable code should not assert one fixed “store-store/store-load” recipe for every JVM/CPU. Use VarHandle acquire/release/opaque modes only when their weaker semantics are deliberately proven and benchmarked.

Final fields receive special initialization safety when construction completes without `this` escaping. That protects the initialized final state; it does not make later mutation of reachable objects thread-safe or safely publish arbitrary non-final state. Keep atomicity, visibility and ordering separate. CAS/ABA details are in (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q3).
:::

- [[b:`synchronized`]]: the simplest intrinsic monitor; automatic release and strong JVM optimization, but no timed/interruptible acquisition or multiple conditions.
- [[b:`ReentrantLock`]]: `tryLock`, timeout, interruptible acquisition, optional fairness, and multiple `Condition`s; always unlock in `finally`.
- [[b:`StampedLock`]]: optimistic reads plus read/write modes; not reentrant and deliberately lower-level.

<pre><code><span class="c">// 1. synchronized — prefer this for a short, unconditional critical section</span>
<span class="k">synchronized</span> (lock) { balance += amount; }
<span class="c">    </span>
<span class="c">// 2. ReentrantLock — timed admission and explicit failure policy</span>
<span class="k">if</span> (lock.<span class="f">tryLock</span>(<span class="n">200</span>, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
    <span class="k">try</span> { balance += amount; } <span class="k">finally</span> { lock.<span class="f">unlock</span>(); }
} <span class="k">else</span> {
    <span class="k">throw</span> <span class="k">new</span> BusyException();
}
<span class="c">    </span>
<span class="c">// 3. StampedLock — copy state, validate, then fall back</span>
<span class="k">long</span> stamp = sl.<span class="f">tryOptimisticRead</span>();
<span class="k">long</span> snapshot = balance;
<span class="k">if</span> (!sl.<span class="f">validate</span>(stamp)) {
    stamp = sl.<span class="f">readLock</span>();
    <span class="k">try</span> { snapshot = balance; } <span class="k">finally</span> { sl.<span class="f">unlockRead</span>(stamp); }
}</code></pre>

:::deep
These mechanisms do [[r:not]] share one implementation: `synchronized` is a JVM intrinsic monitor; `ReentrantLock` is built on `AbstractQueuedSynchronizer` (AQS); `StampedLock` has its own stamped read/write synchronizer. Choose semantics first and benchmark only when contention matters.

Biased locking was disabled in JDK 15 and is no longer a modern selection criterion. The JIT can still coarsen or eliminate locks. Fair `ReentrantLock` reduces starvation risk but can materially reduce throughput.

`StampedLock` optimistic reads must copy the fields needed, then call `validate(stamp)` and retry under a read lock. Do not dereference a mutable object graph before validation. It is not reentrant and has no `Condition`, so it should not be the default.

On JDK 21–23, blocking I/O inside `synchronized` could pin a virtual-thread carrier; JDK 24 removed monitor-related pinning. Use `ReentrantLock` when you need its semantics, not as a blanket JDK 24+ pinning workaround. See the concrete coordination examples in (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q1) and (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q4).
:::

- [[b:Checked]]: recoverable errors, must be declared/handled.
- [[b:Unchecked]] (RuntimeException): programming errors or unrecoverable conditions.

The modern trend (Spring): prefer unchecked.
- avoids `throws` spreading everywhere
- wraps infrastructure errors as domain exceptions
- handles them centrally at the boundary (`@ControllerAdvice`)

**Wrapping correctly — keep the `cause`:**
<pre><code><span class="c">// WRONG: swallows the root cause, the real error's stack trace is gone</span>
<span class="k">try</span> {
    gds.<span class="f">issueTicket</span>(pnr);
} <span class="k">catch</span> (IOException e) {
    <span class="k">throw</span> <span class="k">new</span> TicketingException(<span class="s">"issue failed"</span>);   <span class="r">// e is lost</span>
}
<span class="c">    </span>
<span class="c">// RIGHT: translated into a meaningful business exception but the cause is preserved</span>
<span class="k">try</span> {
    gds.<span class="f">issueTicket</span>(pnr);
} <span class="k">catch</span> (IOException e) {
    <span class="k">throw</span> <span class="k">new</span> TicketingException(<span class="s">"issue failed: "</span> + pnr, e);
}
<span class="c">    </span>
<span class="c">// TicketingException is checked → by default it does NOT roll back, must be declared explicitly</span>
<span class="k">@Transactional</span>(rollbackFor = TicketingException.<span class="k">class</span>)</code></pre>

:::warn Transaction implication
Spring by default only rolls back on unchecked exceptions. A checked exception [[r:does NOT auto-rollback]] without `rollbackFor`.
:::

:::deep
**At the architectural level:** wrap infrastructure errors (SQLException, IOException) into a meaningful *domain exception* (e.g. `InsufficientBalanceException`) → upper layers stay decoupled from technical detail.
- Spring's `@Repository` automatically translates JPA exceptions into `DataAccessException` (unchecked, categorized)
- handle centrally at the boundary via `@ControllerAdvice`, returning *problem+json* (RFC 7807)
- [[r:never swallow an exception]] (catch, log, and ignore) — fail fast

Remember the rollback rule: only unchecked/Error auto-rolls back; checked exceptions must declare `rollbackFor` — a "money didn't roll back" bug that's very common in fintech.
:::
